  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**\n\n   We approach this as a nonnegative sequence minimization problem for the full linear convolution attack surface. The objective function combines the sequence length and the normalized maximum convolution. \n\n   We utilize a two-pronged strategy:\n\n   - **Gradient Projection Optimization (GPO)**: An iterative method that computes gradient directions to reduce the convolution maximum, specifically using the full convolution in full mode as provided.\n   - **Accelerated Gradient Descent (AGD)** with adaptive learning rate and momentum to escape local minima. \n\n   We consider:\n\n   - The sequence must be piecewise uniform due to objective smoothness.\n   - All peaks should be mirrored to achieve optimal \u2018low is better\u2019 results.\n   - The luminance optimization across various peaks while maintaining nonnegative constraints.\n\n   These methods synergize with the previous program's dual-projection optimization, but we focus exclusively on the spoke position normalization.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**\n\n   Let \\( a_0, \\dots, a_{n-1} \\) be the sequence elements. The objective is:\n\n   \\[\n   J(a) = \\frac{2n \\cdot \\|a * a\\|_\\infty}{(\\sum a)^2}\n   \\]\n\n   Where \\( \\|a * a\\|_\\infty \\) is the maximum value of the full linear convolution.\n\n   We model the optimization problem as:\n\n   Minimize \\( J(a) \\)\n\n   Subject to:\n   - \\( a_i \\ge 0 \\) for all \\( i \\)\n   - \\( \\|a\\|_2 \\le 1000 \\) (automatically enforced by clipping via evaluator)\n   - \\( \\sum a = 1 \\) (simplex projection)\n\n   Helper functions to implement gradient projection and attack valley directions.\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**\n\n   - **Phase 1: Initialization**\n     - Start with parent sequence (n=800) or fallback uniform sequence.\n     - Normalize \\( a \\rightarrow a / \\sum a \\)\n     - Clip any negative (prepare for optimizer attack) or >1000 values.\n\n   - **Phase 2: Iterative Optimization**\n     - Use gradient projection per constraint.\n     - Project each length-variation gradient via half-space minimalization.\n     - For each iteration time-bound, apply aggressive weight shifts.\n\n   - **Phase 3: Validation**\n     - Compute exact full convolution for confirmation at each step.\n     - Clip values via evaluate_sequence before computation.\n\n   - **Termination and Fallback**\n     - Upon meeting timeout (1500 seconds), return the best candidate from iterative runs.\n     - Use simplex to keep sum fixed; clip intervals of lengths to [0,1000].\n\n   Detailed Python code ensures simplex projection, gradient estimation, and clip enforcement.\n\n4. **Search Choices with Variations**\n\n   We'll maintain a beam of 5 active candidates from all optimizations. For each candidate, consider:\n\n   - Half-space reduction by 15% if gradient positive\n   - Full-length reduction if time-bound >500 seconds\n   - Double-space merging of identical patterns from previous runs\n   - Keep track of attack surface via std_scores computations\n\n   We'll prioritize initial simplex projections over all but the most recent (delta <0.01) versions.\n\n5. **Compute Budget and Fair Time Slicing**\n\n   We allocate:\n   - 700 seconds for gradient projection phase (warm start)\n   - 800 seconds for simplex-focused optimization\n   - Timers linked to actual objectives (J(x) and gradient descent convergence)\n\n   Use timerspace progression to ensure non-infinite calculations across all iterations.\n\n6. **Validator Registration and Return Contract**\n\n   - Keep up-to-date challenger list: one for each simplex projection\n   - Register sorted clipped vectors with nonnegative sums\n   - Run multiple validations: for simplex, for gradient, for time-bound convergence\n\n   At termination, return best sequence (spyword-valid) clipped to [0,1000] with sum \u22650.01.\n\n   If invalid at transmission, use fallback scoring and adjustments.",
